    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d331">

                <pb facs="0578" n="1538" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1538"/>

                <head>No. 1044.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Kloss to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Swiss Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-09-13"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 13,
                        1887</date>. (Received September 14.)</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Secretary of State</hi>: By note of August 13
                    last, we had the honor to ask you to kindly furnish us as early as possible with
                    as precise explanations as were in your power concerning the extent of the
                    constitutional reservations made by your representatives at the Paris <pb
                        facs="0579" n="1539" xml:id="pg1539"/> Conferences of 1880 and 1883
                    concerning the protection of industrial property.</p>
                <p>In order to inform your Department of the interpretation given to these
                    reservations in certain quarters, our Government transmits to us numbers 5 and
                        8<anchor n="*" xml:id="d331-f1"/> of the “Journal of the Chambers of
                    Commerce” as well as No. 7 of the “Industrial Property,” which treat of this
                    question from different stand-points.</p>
                <p>While taking the liberty to forward with this the pamphlets just mentioned, we
                    avail, etc.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">K. Kloss.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
